Your project is already on MSTest 3.8.2, so constructor injection for `TestContext` is fully supported (available since 3.6+).

Here's the recommended pattern — use the **constructor** for sync setup and `[TestInitialize]` only for async work:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public sealed class MyServiceTests
{
    private readonly TestContext _testContext;
    private readonly MyService _service;

    public MyServiceTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        _testContext = testContext;
        // Synchronous init — enables readonly fields
        _service = new MyService(new FakeRepository());
    }

    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task InitAsync()
    {
        // Async init only — runs after constructor
        await _service.WarmUpAsync(_testContext.CancellationToken);
    }

    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task CleanupAsync()
    {
        await _service.ResetAsync();
    }

    [TestMethod]
    [Timeout(5000)]
    public async Task DoWork_ReturnsExpected()
    {
        var result = await _service.DoWorkAsync(_testContext.CancellationToken);
        Assert.IsNotNull(result);
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

| Concern | Where |
|---------|-------|
| Sync setup (fields, mocks) | Constructor |
| Async setup (DB seeding, HTTP warm-up) | `[TestInitialize]` |
| `TestContext` access | Constructor parameter (enables `readonly`) |
| Cancellation | Flow `_testContext.CancellationToken` into async calls |
| Cleanup | `[TestCleanup]` or implement `IAsyncDisposable` |

**Execution order per test:** Constructor → `[TestInitialize]` → Test method → `[TestCleanup]` → `Dispose`/`DisposeAsync`

This keeps fields `readonly`, satisfies nullability analysis, and cleanly separates sync from async initialization.
